[Music]  here's a question have you ever wondered  whether it would be better for europe to  have the military protection of nato or  to create its own european army yes i  know they are probably compatible but if  you had to choose only one which of the  two options would you go for in this  video we are going to deal with a topic  that has serious implications especially  if we take into account the recent  events on the border between poland and  belarus and let's not forget the  political military crisis between  ukraine and russia a border where  russian forces have spent months making  more and more calculations about a  possible invasion an invasion that  although it does not involve any nato  country at all is seen by many security  experts as well as by the baltic  countries themselves as the prelude the  rehearsal of what could one day happen  for example in estonia latvia or  lithuania so with all this in mind would  you like to know more about europe's  defenses what is in the best interest of  the members of the european union nato  or a new european army developed  completely independently of the united  states which of the two options best  guarantees the interests and security of  the eu countries well let's answer all  these questions listen up  [Music]  how can europe's security be guaranteed  this is the real question but before we  start to dive into the question of  whether or not to stick with nato  whether or not to build a european army  let's briefly look at what exactly nato  is and what it consists of because while  i'm sure the vast majority of you know  about this issue i realize that some of  you may view this whole thing with some  distance  [Music]  nato the north atlantic treaty  organization is an international  military organization whose objective is  to ensure the collective defense of its  member countries it was created in 1949  in the early stages of the cold war and  the dog eat dog confrontation between  the west and the soviet union at that  time and for the next four decades the  threat of war or soviet attack on  western europe was very real precisely  because of this it was decided that the  only way to deter the enemy was to join  forces between the pro-western european  countries and the united states the  basic key to this model is that if any  enemy attacks any of the nato member  countries the rest commit themselves to  assist that country militarily give them  aid and even intervene directly it's a  model that has survived over time but  has seen the name of its main adversary  change from the soviet union to russia  however it is not only a commitment to  mutual defense nato also seeks to  improve the modernization coordination  and integration of the different armies  in order to be able to fight together if  necessary we are talking about a  complete security umbrella decision  making making commitments and even  deploying troops to reinforce the  security of the weakest allies this is  for example what happens with the forces  that many member countries of this  alliance sends to the baltic countries  we could also include permanent or  rotational deployments of u.s forces in  alliance member countries in terms of  security it sounds good doesn't it  integration coordination and the support  of the world's largest superpower if  we're talking about security what more  else could you ask for  [Music]  well it turns out there is one small  problem over the years nato seems to  have become less popular amongst the old  powers of western europe for example pay  close attention to these statements by  the french president on the crisis with  russia  it's good that europeans and the united  states coordinate but it's necessary  that europeans conduct their own  dialogue emmanuel macron president of  france addressing the european  parliament that's right basically what  macron is asking is that europe's  security policy be completely  independent from that of the united  states that brussels conducts its  negotiations agreements and commitments  completely independently of washington  even in cases of conflict if we stop to  think about it this means breaking the  common front the atlantic axis the idea  that underlies nato itself  and obviously in order to be independent  in security matters you need to have  your own military capabilities what i  mean is that clearly you don't want to  be making decisions while you're  covering yourself with other people's  soldiers and so visualpolitik friends it  is not at all common that a european  leader of such influence speaks with  such a degree of nato skepticism in fact  listening to macron we could almost come  to think i don't know that vladimir  putin was on the verge of getting away  with fracturing nato by threat by the  way if you want to learn more details  about how nato has changed over time and  how it functions you can't miss this  video  [Music]  and i'll also link it in the description  [Music]  the guardian of europe  let's see have you ever wondered what  europe's defense is like the current  system and what role the united states  plays in it well the armies belonging to  nato have hundreds and hundreds of  thousands of soldiers in old europe of  course the vast majority of them belong  logically to the european countries  themselves however we are talking about  armies that typically have had many  shortcomings particularly if we refer to  their deployment capabilities something  that greatly limits their operations  [Music]  and this is not just our opinion it's  what the european union itself says in  its draft for the creation of a european  rapid response military force  we need to be able to respond to  imminent threats or quickly react to a  crisis situation for example a rescue  and evacuation mission or a  stabilization operation in a hostile  environment eu strategic compass draft  evidently it raises this as an objective  because the eu is aware that these days  its european countries do not have this  capacity the fact is that because of all  these shortcomings every time tension  rises in eastern europe as for example  happened after russia's invasion and  subsequent annexation of crimea the  united states is forced to make a move  and reinforce its presence on the  continent the u.s power is one of the  few members perhaps along with the  united kingdom and perhaps france that  could make very rapid deployments in  fact european countries have such  limited capabilities that on their own  without u.s support and assistance it is  very likely that they would not be able  to guarantee the security of all member  countries so much so that the barack  obama administration created the  european deterrence initiative known as  edi for short in 2014.  [Music]  this initiative responded to a  long-standing request from the countries  of eastern europe it was intended to  reinforce the u.s contribution to  european security to achieve this  objective the initiative proposed to  increase the forces deployed in country  is considered to be on the front line  such as estonia latvia lithuania and  poland also to increase the budget for  multi-national military exercises and  improve key infrastructure for  deterrence in practice the program has  cost americans nearly 25 billion dollars  between 2016 and 2021. quite a show of  commitment on the part of uncle sam  thanks to the european deterrence  initiative the united states has  deployed f-15 and f-16 fighters in the  united kingdom and italy it has also  increased the number of troops deployed  in poland and the baltic countries now  the question is is this reinforcement  sufficient to protect europe from  external aggression well yes and no at  the same time yes because thanks to  these deployments any aggression would  put the united states itself in play not  only in terms of the general deterrence  implied by the mutual security  commitment but also the prospect of  having to attack u.s troops themselves  and that visualpolitik fans is a serious  call it is what we could call deterrence  of no return  [Music]  but on the other hand experts in  military strategy seem to have been very  very clear about one thing the three  baltic republics which also have a large  russian minority amongst their  population are nato's achilles heel  let's be clear according to studies and  simulations russia could reach the  baltic capitals talin riga and vilnius  in a maximum of 60 days in other words  and take note because this is a vital  issue in the european army debate the  general deterrence discourse that nato  is capable of championing is the only  real protection that these countries  have in practice against an invasion or  a hybrid warfare attack that is perhaps  what explains new stories like these  kalas estonian prime minister calls for  u.s troops in the baltics  uk considers doubling troop presence in  estonia the question is why is estonia  asking united states and the united  kingdom for more troops and not doing  the same with allied european countries  that are much closer  [Music]  well basically they're doing it as a  matter of capability and deterrence in  the end the great asset that nato has  and that for example a strictly european  union security organization would never  have is that the united states is behind  it and to a lesser extent also the  united kingdom the largest and one of  the next largest military powers in the  world nor would this same european  organization have the combat and rapid  deployment capabilities that the united  states currently brings to nato and this  is something that brings us directly to  another one of the big issues cash as  you all know nato has a spending  guideline of two 2 of gdp however with  the exception of the united states the  united kingdom the baltic republics  poland and a few other exceptions the  vast majority of the members of the  atlantic club do not comply with this  guideline and the question is would they  be willing to change the allocation of  public funds to increase their military  budgets well in view of the above  everything seems to point to the fact  that the answer is negative  [Music]  take note because it's not just a  question of the volume of spending but  also of its breakdown for example  european nato countries currently spend  almost as much on defense as china and  much much more than russia the problem  is not the volume of expenditure but  above all it's composition for example  spending too much on personnel and too  little on equipment in short the armies  of european union countries with a few  honorable exceptions do not have the  capacity to be considered relevant  players on their own even if we analyze  them as a whole  [Applause]  and of course without effective armed  forces and without even being able to  guarantee security in continental europe  how on earth are you going to be able to  have a totally independent security  policy how are you going to establish an  effective deterrence discourse and as  far as other issues such as energy  dependence go let's not even talk about  it however would the creation of a  strictly european military defense  structure as mccron seems to want to  propose solve all of these problems is  nato to blame for the lack of commitment  shown by many of its member countries  these are the key questions in this  whole story listen up  [Music]  the illusion of self-defense  why is macron calling for europe to go  with it alone does the european union  really need to move forward with  military structures other than nato  would europe be better defended if it  relied solely on its own resources and  did without the united states well you  see in this case the answers are almost  always political in nature  [Music]  donald trump's presidency in the united  states for example only fueled the  sentiment of wanting to bet the future  of security on genuinely european armed  forces why well let's just say trump  wasn't the friendliest to nato and  european countries  trump discussed pulling u.s from nato  aids say amid new concerns over russia  it's not right to be taken advantage of  on nato and also then to be taken  advantage of on trade and that's what  happens we can't let that happen donald  trump  we don't want to be the suckers anymore  we are reducing the force because  they're not paying their bills it's very  simple donald trump  the u.s to withdraw about 12 000 troops  from germany but nearly half to stay in  europe in the end none of this happened  but words are rarely neutral be there as  it may the idea of putting in place a  european army or a coordination decision  and operation structure without the  united states united kingdom and canada  is not new but thanks to trump and  perhaps to growing energy dependence on  russia we have indeed encountered a new  enthusiasm of course there have already  been many attempts and many failures by  the european union it has never been  possible to get things right  this is the case for example of the euro  corps which with up to 60 000 troops and  11 participating states is a military  corps established in 1992 which serves  primarily to support un nato and osce  peace or stabilization missions there  are also other examples such as the  european gender mary force which is a  kind of european police force with  military status that has only 2 000  units attached to it or the european  rapid task force which hasn't existed  since 2012 and which was supposed to be  a small rapid deployment force capable  of maneuvering in less than five days to  contribute to humanitarian and  peacekeeping missions today all these  corpse and some additional ones are  framed within the permanent structured  cooperation known as pesco which seeks  the structural integration of the armed  forces of the 25 member states that are  attached to it  could this really change the future  could this reality change in the future  well it's possible but the first to be  suspicious of this whole story are the  countries of the central and eastern  europe the former members of the warsaw  pact countries such as poland the baltic  republics romania bulgaria and slovakia  feel much more secure with washington  than they do with brussels at all levels  the crisis with ukraine was a good  example of this while paris and berlin  wanted to conduct their own negotiations  with putin most eastern european  countries ask for two things a single  joint nato voice and an increased  deployment of u.s troops  it is very important for nato to give a  common response to the current attention  general rajmund andra jazak chief of the  general staff of the armed forces of  poland in fact this could be an  important element of fracture a further  crack if france and germany remains  determined to go at it alone of course  recent developments with russia seem to  have achieved quite the opposite of what  macron and also vladimir putin intended  the crisis in ukraine has rejuvenated  the north atlantic treaty organization  which has been able to show its  importance in a way it had not done in  decades what's more improving  coordination and interoperability of  european armies is desirable but trying  to develop a structure that ends up  rubbing up against the atlantic alliance  could be a terrible idea but at this  point it's your turn how do you think  europe would be better defended with a  european army or with nato under  washington's umbrella leave us your  opinions below in the comments and  remember if you liked this video don't  forget to like and subscribe to our  channel all the best and i'll see you  next time  [Music]  you

